C. Correct. “Does this belief or practice have a particular meaning for you or your family?” This question invites the client to describe personal meaning without assuming that the client represents an entire cultural group. It is clinically relevant, open-ended, and focused on the individual client’s experience. Respectful curiosity helps the therapist gather information while preserving dignity and avoiding stereotypes. Memory cue: Strong cultural questions usually ask: “What does this mean to you?” Weak questions assume: “This must mean the same thing for everyone in your group.”

